// gunbc.host.network_install_ci — the CI entry for the one-shot network install, and nothing else.
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS SEPARATELY. dag/gunbc/ci/ci_spec.dag registers this file as the wet target for the
// install step (`network_install_ci_wet`, delegating to `network_install_ci_run` below). dag/gunbc/host/
// network_install.dag is the install workflow — gates, ports, fold, journal — and an entry that opens a real
// ssh agent, reads the GitHub environment and writes a receipt does not belong in it: that file would then have
// to import this one, and this one already imports it for the port and outcome types. So the ledger that
// declares the workflow keeps its gates pure, and everything that TOUCHES the machine sits where a reviewer can
// read it in one screen.
//
// WHAT IT DOES, in the order it does it:
//   1. ONE observer clock reading. Every instant this entry uses — the run's decided_at, the subject's
//      started_at, the reader's observed_at — is that one instant. An unreadable clock is an exit failure:
//      there is no default, no zero, no `now` substituted later. (DESIGN.md Sec 26: the observer's word is
//      not an input to be guessed.)
//   2. THE DISPATCH SUBJECT, from the workflow environment, the same variable every other single-host wet
//      entry in this fleet reads (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge fleet_converge_expected_host_env_name). One host
//      per run; nothing is inferred from the host list.
//   3. EVERY COMMITTED INPUT BY LOOKUP. The arrival population row, the managed-host list, the baseboard (as
//      the write-controller row declares it), the access observation and the BmcSecureStanding (through the
//      managed-host binding, which is where the fleet commits them), the write-controller row, the firmware
//      release capability row, the qualification, the secret-ref password file and the dispatch id. No input
//      in this entry is a literal, and no input is skipped: a missing row refuses with its own typed, located
//      cause naming the ledger it should have come from.
//   4. network_install_run_and_release — the ONLY run entry. `network_install_run` is never called from here:
//      it returns the live durable hold to its caller, and an entry with no caller leaks it.
//
// WHY srv15 CANNOT BE DISPATCHED TODAY, and what that refusal says. srv15 (bobo) has a committed NIC row, a
// committed dated target-disk row, a committed baseboard and a committed write-controller row. What it does
// NOT have is a committed BmcAccessObservationStanding or BmcSecureStanding: `bmc_secure` keeps that frontier
// unbound until a live account-state read commits its receipt (frontier 1d-c), and the same live arrival run
// that mints them also adds the unit's arrival population row. So the honest wet path today is the refusal in
// `network_install_ci_missing_inputs` — typed, located, naming the missing rows and the 1d-c dependency. That
// is a refusal that tells the operator the truth, not a scaffold: this entry does not mint a standing, does
// not read a witness fixture, and does not relax the standing requirement. The witness supplies srv15's
// standing through derive_bmc_secure for the ADMITTED path; production reads the committed one, and today
// there is none.
//
// THE BMC CREDENTIAL NEVER APPEARS ON ARGV AND NEVER APPEARS IN THE LOG. The entry reads only a PATH from the
// environment, requires it to be a readable 0600 file (gunbc.auth.private_secret_file), and hands the path to
// the run, which opens it itself. The receipt names the path and never its bytes.
module gunbc.host.network_install_ci

